+/* REAPER-REMOVED-LOG (v3.8.4) - ONE-BETA SAFETY NET: DELETE THIS BLOCK AND ITS ONE CALL AFTER ONE STABLE VERSION.
+ * The request addresses below were removed because no shipped page, open code or closed program called them
+ * (REAPER-XSITE-OWNOUT, REAPER-UNUSED-V384, REAPER-UNUSED-V384B). Should something still ask for one - an add-on, a
+ * script, an old bookmark - the request falls through the handler table to the same 404 as any unknown name, and
+ * this writes one system-log line so the caller can be found: the table's own name and the client address, never
+ * the query string or the body. One line per name per boot: a byte per name in a root-only file under /tmp is
+ * shared by both listeners and outlives an httpd restart, so a LAN client cannot fill the log (one line for each
+ * name below at most). It runs where no handler matched - no login check exists for such a request - so a
+ * signed-in and a signed-out client get the same answer and leave the same line.
+ * Each name is kept in TWO PIECES (its first four characters, then the rest) on purpose: the image check proves a
+ * removed address by the absence of its string in httpd (verify_markers "usr/sbin/httpd|!name.cgi"), and a whole
+ * name here would make every such line fail. Keep the split when adding a name. */
+#define REAPER_REMOVED_SEEN	"/tmp/reaper/httpd.removed"
+static const struct { const char *head, *rest; } reaper_removed_addr[] = {
+	/* 2026-11-17 */
+	{ "rene", "w_ikev2_cert_mobile.pem" }, { "rene", "w_ikev2_cert_windows.der" },
